# Git

A question is not permission. `reset`, `rebase`, `push --force`, `push --force-with-lease`, `branch -D`, `clean`, `checkout -- <path>`, and `worktree remove --force` run only on an order that names the operation and the target SHA or ref. That holds for the agent's own uncommitted edit too: undo it with an edit. Before removing any worktree, run `git -C <worktree> status --short`; undo what it lists, then remove it without `--force`, from outside the worktree.

After a squash merge, `git branch -d <branch>` refuses to delete the local branch, because `main` holds no commit of it. Keep the branch and ask the owner, with the branch name and its SHA; `-D` is not the next step. After PR #381 merged, the local branch `fix/327-audit-cut-off` (23242f0) was deleted with `-D` without an order. Its commit stayed reachable at `refs/pull/381/head`.

A conflict that another PR's merge caused is solved by merging `origin/main` into the branch (`git merge origin/main`), without asking: nothing is rewritten, and the reviewed commits stay. A rebase and a force-push still need an order. Until 2026-11-13 either way was asked first; with rows in parallel every second merge conflicts in the roadmap and the design tables (the owner's decision, Issue #372, after PR #367).

Such a conflict keeps both sides. Where two PRs added at the same place (the end of design §20.7, the end of `docs/design-decisions.md`, the list of done rows in `docs/roadmap.md`), `main`'s part comes first and the branch's part after it. Table rows are matched by their ID: a row that `main` has takes `main`'s form, and a row that the branch added stays as it is. Where both sides added to one row or one method, both additions stay. Git may pair unrelated lines of tests that both sides appended, such as doc comments and closing braces: keep every test of both sides, and compare the counts of `[Fact]` and `[Theory]` before and after. After PR #377 merged, `origin/main` was merged into the branches of PRs #381, #382, #384, #387, #388, #390, and #391 this way: both PRs' additions to one table row of `docs/guide/reference/logs.md` were kept, both checks in `src/ElasticLM.Licensing/Limits.cs` (PR #382), and every test of two test files in PR #391.

After a conflict in code, every test of the projects the merge touched runs on the merged tree before the push, as for `Limits.cs` in PR #382. The merged tree is built even when git reports no conflict. In PR #390 a new test of the branch did not set the required member `LeaseSpec.AnsweredProofId`, which V1d5 had added on `main`; git reported no conflict, and the build failed with CS9035. After a failed build, `dotnet test --no-build` runs the earlier binaries, so its result is not used.

When asked about such an operation, answer with the current `HEAD` SHA, the target SHA, what would disappear, and the exact command. Then wait.

- Finished work goes to a PR in the same turn, without waiting for an ask: commit on the Issue's branch, push it, and open the PR (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`). The owner reviews on the PR; uncommitted changes on this machine cannot be reviewed. Do not stop at "may I commit?".
- Long work runs in an agent worktree (`git worktree add .dev/<branch> <branch>`), not in the main working tree: the owner may switch that tree to another branch to review.
- This covers the Issue's own branch only. Never commit to `main` or push to it, and never merge.
- A squash merge that the owner orders (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, step 7) names the head it merges and carries its own message: `gh pr merge <n> --squash --match-head-commit <sha> --subject "<PR title> (#<n>)" --body <text>`, where the text is a summary, a line `Refs #<issue>`, and the `Co-Authored-By` line. The message's body holds no closing verb: the `Closes` of the PR body closes the Issue (`.agents/skills/github-closing/SKILL.md`). The SHA for `--match-head-commit` is copied from the output of `git rev-parse HEAD`. On 2026-11-13 the owner ordered the merges of PRs #381, #382, and #384. The squash body of PR #381 held `Closes #327`, while those of PR #382 and PR #384 used `Refs`; and the first merge command of PR #381 passed a SHA whose last digits were made up, which GitHub refused.
- `git branch --show-current` is part of the command that commits. A chain that creates a branch can fail before it does, and the commit then lands on `main`.
- A push is chained to the checks that `.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc` (Gates) asks before it, so that a failed check stops the push (`<checks>; if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) { git push }`).
- Before a commit, read `git status --short` and stage the files the change meant to touch; a file nobody meant to add is deleted, not committed. `git add -A` committed an empty `{}` (be8b64a) and an empty `devices-and-proofs.md` (d14c441) at the repository root, left by a mistyped shell redirection; each stayed on `main` until someone saw it. `git add -A` was used once more in the work of PR #377.
- Read `gh pr view <n> --json state` before pushing to a branch that has a PR, and before editing its body. A push to the branch of a merged PR recreates the deleted branch and reaches nothing; an edited body no longer matches what was merged. After PR #201 merged, a second commit was pushed and the body edited to describe it; the commit moved to the next PR and the body was restored. The read and the edit are one command that stops on the value: `test "$(gh pr view <n> --json state --jq .state)" = OPEN && gh pr edit <n> …`. A chain `gh pr view <n> --json state --jq .state && gh pr edit <n> …` goes on whatever the state is, because the first command succeeds: after PR #362 merged it printed `MERGED` and replaced the body, which was then put back.
- Do not merge the branch of a PR that has not merged into another branch. Wait for that PR, or open the second PR on top of it (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, Stacked PRs). After the first PR merges, the two histories have two merge bases; local git merges them cleanly (`git merge-tree`), while GitHub shows the second PR as conflicting and starts no `pull_request` run. In PR #367 the branch of PR #362 was merged in to set its roadmap row to `done`; once PR #362 had merged, PR #367 read `CONFLICTING`, and the owner chose to merge `main` into the branch.
- To push the earlier of two local commits and keep the later one local, push that commit by its SHA: `git push origin <sha>:refs/heads/<branch>`. When the commit to push comes after the one to keep, put them in order on a new branch first (`git branch <new> <base>`, then `git cherry-pick` each), check that the two branches hold the same tree (`git diff <old> <new>` prints nothing), and push from it. In V1c4a a fix to a measurement workflow was committed on top of the English pages, which could not be pushed before the Japanese pages; reordering and pushing the fix by its SHA sent only it (PR #249).
- If history was rewritten, put the SHA from before the rewrite on the first line of the reply.
- Never skip hooks (`--no-verify`) or signing unless the owner orders it.
- Never commit key material, a real license token, or a secret, test fixtures included (`.cursor/rules/security.mdc`). Read `git status --short` and the staged file list before each commit.
